Verdict: PASS. I wrote it to session_plan/eval_task_1.md.

I ran the built binary in an empty temporary directory and in this repo:

| Form | Exit | What it prints now |
|---|---|---|
| yoyo watch | 2 | Refusal on stderr. It names the /watch line to use in a session, plus a hint about auto_watch. In this repo the hint names the detected cargo command. |
| yoyo watch "echo hi" | 2 | Same refusal, with /watch echo hi as the session line. |
| yoyo watch off | 2 | Refusal, plus advice to leave auto_watch unset or false. |
| yoyo watch status | 0 | Says nothing is on in this process and what auto_watch would do in a session started there. |

None of these print "Watch mode ON" any more.

• The `auto_watch` hint is accurate. Its cases (nothing detected, tool not on PATH, armed) match what arm_auto_watch in src/watch.rs actually does. It never claims auto_watch takes a command of its own.
• The REPL is untouched. src/watch.rs is not in the diff, so /watch inside a session behaves exactly as before.
• Nothing is left unused. The new src/watch_shell.rs is registered in main.rs and called from src/dispatch_sub.rs. Its exit code reuses the existing code 2 that yoyo extended and yoyo setup use. The only remaining #1088 mentions in src, docs and skills are code comments, nothing a user sees.
• The docs are updated. In docs/src/usage/commands.md, both the command-table row and the exit-code paragraph are rewritten, and the watch line in yoyo --help (src/help.rs) changed to match.

One thing to know: the existing integration test for #1009 was rewritten rather than kept as it was. Its assertions expected "Watch mode ON" with exit 0, which is exactly the behaviour #1088 removes, so it couldn't stay. The new version still checks the #1009 quoting rule, now through the /watch … line in the refusal.
